  {
    "raw_asr": "uh basically we need to update the esp thirty two dual core microcontroller firmware over serial bootloader using esp tool dot py without corrupting calibration tables follow these operational procedures first erase the designated application partition by running esp tool dot py dash dash port slash dev slash tty u s b zero erase region zero x one zero zero zero zero zero x one zero zero zero zero zero to clear stale flash blocks second flash the newly compiled application binary image with esp tool dot py dash dash chip esp thirty two dash dash port slash dev slash tty u s b zero dash dash baud nine two one six zero zero right flash zero x one zero zero zero zero app dot bin using high speed transfer third verify checksum integrity against the partition table using esp tool dot py dash dash port slash dev slash tty u s b zero verify flash zero x one zero zero zero zero app dot bin fourth trigger a hardware system reset via d t r pin toggling to boot into the updated application firmware runtime cleanly and inspect bootloader output for critical startup error codes and boot messages",
    "refined_text": "Update the ESP32 dual-core microcontroller firmware over the serial bootloader using `esptool.py` without corrupting active calibration tables. Follow these operational procedures to safely flash and verify the application binary:\n\n1. Erase the designated application partition by executing `esptool.py --port /dev/ttyUSB0 erase_region 0x10000 0x100000` to clear stale flash blocks cleanly.\n2. Flash the newly compiled application binary image with `esptool.py --chip esp32 --port /dev/ttyUSB0 --baud 921600 write_flash 0x10000 app.bin` using high-speed UART transfer protocols.\n3. Verify checksum integrity against the partition table using `esptool.py --port /dev/ttyUSB0 verify_flash 0x10000 app.bin` to detect corrupted flash sectors across storage.\n4. Trigger a hardware system reset via DTR pin toggling to boot into the updated application firmware runtime cleanly, and inspect bootloader output for critical system startup error codes and boot messages."
  },
  {
    "raw_asr": "so initialize the stm thirty two f four bare metal system clock tree from the external eight megahertz high speed crystal oscillator up to one hundred sixty eight megahertz maximum operational frequency execute these hardware configuration steps in exact sequential order first write one to the h s e o n bit in register r c c dash c r and poll the h s e r d y flag until the external crystal oscillator stabilizes reliably across system power domains second set flash memory latency wait states to five in register f l a s h dash a c r to prevent instruction prefetch memory access violations at high core clock frequencies third configure phase locked loop prescalers in register r c c dash p l l c f g r with p l l m equals eight p l l n equals three three six and p l l p equals two to generate the high speed internal system frequency fourth enable the p l l and switch the system clock multiplexer to the p l l output in register r c c dash c f g r to complete core clock initialization successfully across hardware peripherals and bus bridges",
    "refined_text": "Initialize the STM32F4 bare-metal system clock tree from the external 8MHz high-speed crystal oscillator up to 168MHz maximum operational frequency. Execute these hardware configuration steps in exact sequential order:\n\n1. Write 1 to the `HSEON` bit in register `RCC_CR` and poll the `HSERDY` flag until the external crystal oscillator stabilizes reliably across system power domains.\n2. Set flash memory latency wait states to 5 in register `FLASH_ACR` to prevent instruction prefetch memory access violations at high core clock frequencies.\n3. Configure Phase-Locked Loop prescalers in register `RCC_PLLCFGR` with `PLLM=8`, `PLLN=336`, and `PLLP=2` to generate the high-speed internal system frequency.\n4. Enable the PLL and switch the system clock multiplexer to the PLL output in register `RCC_CFGR` to complete core clock initialization successfully across hardware peripherals and bus bridges in silicon."
  },
  {
    "raw_asr": "um configure the real time task scheduler in free are toss to manage telemetry sampling and motor actuation threads without priority inversion or stack overflow follow these engineering steps to initialize task concurrency first allocate dedicated task control blocks and thread stacks using x task create static with a stack depth of five hundred twelve words four the high priority control task second create a thread safe message queue using x queue create with depth ten and item size thirty two bytes four transferring sensor telemetry between threads reliably third assign priority four to the time critical motor loop and priority two to the background telemetry reporter to enforce deterministic scheduling across execution fourth call v task start scheduler to initiate preemption and monitor remaining stack memory with u x task get stack high water mark during execution to avoid memory corruption across threads and kernel heaps in physical system ram",
    "refined_text": "Configure the real-time task scheduler in FreeRTOS to manage telemetry sampling and motor actuation threads without priority inversion or stack overflow. Follow these engineering steps to initialize task concurrency:\n\n1. Allocate dedicated task control blocks and thread stacks using `xTaskCreateStatic()` with a stack depth of 512 words for the high-priority control task.\n2. Create a thread-safe message queue using `xQueueCreate()` with depth 10 and item size 32 bytes for transferring sensor telemetry between threads reliably.\n3. Assign priority 4 to the time-critical motor loop and priority 2 to the background telemetry reporter to enforce deterministic scheduling across execution.\n4. Call `vTaskStartScheduler()` to initiate preemption and monitor remaining stack memory with `uxTaskGetStackHighWaterMark()` during execution to avoid memory corruption across threads and active kernel heaps in physical system RAM."
  },
  {
    "raw_asr": "integrate an out of tree i two c digital accelerometer sensor driver into zephyr r t o s four the n r f five two target board follow these implementation steps to establish the hardware abstraction binding first define the peripheral hardware node inside the board devicetree overlay file with compatible string vendor comma sensor and i two c bus address zero x one d with proper pull up resistor pin assignments second enable the matching kconfig driver symbols buy setting config sensor equals y and config accel driver equals y in the prj dot c o n f project configuration file third implement the driver initialization api binding using device and api init with post kernel initialization priority level to register the device object cleanly fourth verify sensor data polling in the application loop using sensor sample fetch and sensor channel get to read three axis acceleration values cleanly without bus stalls or dropped samples during continuous runtime execution across all peripheral acquisition cycles",
    "refined_text": "Integrate an out-of-tree I2C digital accelerometer sensor driver into Zephyr RTOS for the nRF52 target board. Follow these implementation steps to establish the hardware abstraction binding:\n\n1. Define the peripheral hardware node inside the board Devicetree overlay file with compatible string \"vendor,sensor\" and I2C bus address `0x1D` with proper pull-up resistor pin assignments.\n2. Enable the matching Kconfig driver symbols by setting `CONFIG_SENSOR=y` and `CONFIG_ACCEL_DRIVER=y` in the `prj.conf` project configuration file.\n3. Implement the driver initialization API binding using `DEVICE_AND_API_INIT()` with post-kernel initialization priority level to register the device object cleanly.\n4. Verify sensor data polling in the application loop using `sensor_sample_fetch()` and `sensor_channel_get()` to read three-axis acceleration values cleanly without bus stalls or dropped samples during continuous runtime execution across all peripheral acquisition cycles."
  },
