    "refined_text": "Configuring an industrial USB video class camera on Linux edge nodes requires establishing low-level V4L2 kernel controls before initiating application capture loops.\n1. Inspect hardware supported pixel formats, continuous frame rates, and supported resolutions using `v4l2-ctl --list-formats-ext -d /dev/video0`.\n2. Configure driver capture format to uncompressed YUYV with 1080p resolution at 60 FPS using `v4l2-ctl -d /dev/video0 --set-fmt-video=width=1920,height=1080,pixelformat=YUYV`.\n3. Disable automatic gain and auto exposure while locking manual shutter time using `v4l2-ctl -d /dev/video0 -c exposure_auto=1,exposure_absolute=300`.\n4. Stream memory-mapped video buffers directly into an OpenCV capture loop using `cv2.VideoCapture(0, cv2.CAP_V4L2)` without kernel buffer copy overhead to minimize frame latency during real-time processing."
