  {
    "raw_asr": "follow this low latency kernel and jack configuration procedure to eliminate buffer underruns during high channel count audio tracking one grant real time schedulin priority and unlimited memory locking to the audio group buy adding at audio dash r t p r i o ninety five and at audio dash memlock unlimited to slash etc slash security slash limits dot d slash audio dot conf two configure the c p u governor to maximum performance across all cores using c p u power frequency dash set dash g performance to prevent clock throttling latencies three launch the jack sound daemon with al sa driver parameters set to forty eight kilohertz one two eight frames period and three buffer periods via jack d dash r dash p ninety five dash d alsa dash d h w zero dash r forty eight thousand dash p one two eight dash n three four verify zero hardware x run events under peak d s p synthesizer load using jack underscore c p u underscore load and p w dash top",
    "refined_text": "Follow this low-latency kernel and JACK configuration procedure to eliminate buffer underruns during high-channel-count audio tracking.\n1. Grant real-time scheduling priority and unlimited memory locking to the audio group by adding `@audio - rtprio 95` and `@audio - memlock unlimited` to `/etc/security/limits.d/audio.conf`.\n2. Configure the CPU governor to maximum performance across all cores using `cpupower frequency-set -g performance` to prevent clock throttling latencies.\n3. Launch the JACK sound daemon with ALSA driver parameters set to 48kHz, 128 frames period, and 3 buffer periods via `jackd -R -P 95 -d alsa -d hw:0 -r 48000 -p 128 -n 3`.\n4. Verify zero hardware XRUN events under peak DSP synthesizer load using `jack_cpu_load` and `pw-top`."
  },
  {
    "raw_asr": "follow this mathematical implementation procedure to synthesize a second order peaking biquad equalizer filter four digital signal processing one compute angular frequency w zero equals two times m underscore p i times f zero slash f s and intermediate bandwidth parameter alpha equals sine of w zero divided buy two times q using target center frequency f zero and samplin rate f s two derive filter gain factor a equals ten raised to d b gain slash forty and calculate unnormalized feedforward coefficients b zero equals one plus alpha times a b one equals minus two times cosine of w zero and b two equals one minus alpha times a three calculate feedback coefficients a zero equals one plus alpha slash a a one equals minus two times cosine of w zero and a two equals one minus alpha slash a four normalize all coefficients buy dividing with a zero and execute the direct form two transposed difference equation across the incoming sample buffer",
    "refined_text": "Follow this mathematical implementation procedure to synthesize a second-order peaking biquad equalizer filter for digital signal processing.\n1. Compute angular frequency `w0 = 2 * M_PI * f0 / Fs` and intermediate bandwidth parameter `alpha = sin(w0) / (2 * Q)` using target center frequency `f0` and sampling rate `Fs`.\n2. Derive filter gain factor `A = 10^(dBgain / 40)` and calculate unnormalized feedforward coefficients `b0 = 1 + alpha * A`, `b1 = -2 * cos(w0)`, and `b2 = 1 - alpha * A`.\n3. Calculate feedback coefficients `a0 = 1 + alpha / A`, `a1 = -2 * cos(w0)`, and `a2 = 1 - alpha / A`.\n4. Normalize all coefficients by dividing with `a0` and execute the Direct Form II transposed difference equation across the incoming sample buffer."
  },
  {
    "raw_asr": "follow this two pass processing workflow to normalize broadcast audio tracks to standard e b u r one two eight and i t u dash r b s dot one seven seven zero specifications one perform an initial acoustic measurement pass using f f m peg dash i input dot w a v dash a f loudnorm equals i equals minus sixteen colon t p equals minus one point five colon l r a equals eleven colon print underscore format equals json dash f null dash to extract integrated loudness loudness range and true peak values two parse the measured json telemetry to obtain measured underscore i measured underscore t p measured underscore l r a and measured underscore thresh values from the analyzer output three execute the second linear normaliz pass buy passing the measured metrics back into the loudnorm filter with linear equals true to prevent dynamic compression pumping four export the broadcast compliant master file to twenty four bit forty eight kilohertz p c m using f f m peg dash i input dot w a v dash a f loudnorm equals linear equals true colon measured underscore i equals minus eighteen colon measured underscore t p equals minus one point five dash c colon a pcm underscore s two four l e master dot w a v",
    "refined_text": "Follow this two-pass processing workflow to normalize broadcast audio tracks to standard EBU R128 and ITU-R BS.1770 specifications.\n1. Perform an initial acoustic measurement pass using `ffmpeg -i input.wav -af loudnorm=I=-16:TP=-1.5:LRA=11:print_format=json -f null -` to extract integrated loudness, loudness range, and true peak values.\n2. Parse the measured JSON telemetry to obtain `measured_I`, `measured_TP`, `measured_LRA`, and `measured_thresh` values from the analyzer output.\n3. Execute the second linear normalization pass by passing the measured metrics back into the `loudnorm` filter with `linear=true` to prevent dynamic compression pumping.\n4. Export the broadcast-compliant master file to 24-bit 48kHz PCM using `ffmpeg -i input.wav -af loudnorm=linear=true:measured_I=-18:measured_TP=-1.5 -c:a pcm_s24le master.wav`."
