    "raw_asr": "uh to tune the linux operating system kernel for write heavy database workloads and eliminate synchronous dirty page flushing pauses apply this kernel configuration plan effectively across database servers first configure vm dot dirty background ratio equals five and vm dot dirty ratio equals ten using sysctl dash w to prompt kernel writeback daemons to flush dirty pages continuously without stalling client processes second shorten dirty page expiration lifetimes by setting vm dot dirty expire centisecs equals one zero zero zero and vm dot dirty writeback centisecs equals five zero zero to flush dirty memory every five seconds third instruct the database engine to issue posix fadvise with posix fadvise dontneed immediately after reading large s s tables to prevent cache eviction of critical b tree index pages from resident memory",
    "refined_text": "To tune the Linux operating system kernel for write-heavy database workloads and eliminate synchronous dirty page flushing pauses, apply this kernel configuration plan effectively across database servers.\n\n1. Configure `vm.dirty_background_ratio = 5` and `vm.dirty_ratio = 10` using `sysctl -w` to prompt kernel writeback daemons to flush dirty pages continuously without stalling client processes.\n2. Shorten dirty page expiration lifetimes by setting `vm.dirty_expire_centisecs = 1000` and `vm.dirty_writeback_centisecs = 500` to flush dirty memory every 5 seconds.\n3. Instruct the database engine to issue `posix_fadvise()` with `POSIX_FADV_DONTNEED` immediately after reading large SSTables to prevent cache eviction of critical B-tree index pages from resident memory."
  },
